The board state has two solid, unspoilable winning positions that can be realized under gravity: \((3,5)\) for \(x\) (completing the horizontal three already present in row 3, columns 2-4) and \((2,5)\) for \(o\) (completing the horizontal three already present in row 2, columns 2-4). All other potential fours are either already blocked by an opposing piece or require long, spoilable vertical builds.

Column 5 is currently empty, so its free cell is row 5. The single move that most advances the only solid threat belonging to \(x\) is therefore to occupy that cell. After \(x\) plays column 5:

- the free cell becomes row 4;
- a second stone by \(x\) in the same column makes row 3 free and thereby turns \((3,5)\) into an immediate threat;
- \(o\) is then forced to answer on column 5 (otherwise \(x\) wins on the next turn);
- the forced reply by \(o\) itself frees row 2, creating \(o\)'s own threat, which \(x\) can simply block.

No other legal column produces an analogous forced sequence. Columns 1 and 4 merely defuse \(o\)'s only easy (top-row) threat pair; they create only dead threes for \(x\) and leave the critical column 5 untouched. Columns 0 and 6 begin spoilable verticals that \(o\) can break on any free turn. Consequently the unique optimal opening is column 5: it seizes the initiative on the sole unspoilable threat line while simultaneously restricting \(o\)'s counter-play on the same column.

Final Answer: 5

